---
/**
 * The cover for "Surfing Dog Inbox is open", drawing the post in four beats.
 *
 *  1. Two kinds of sender, one door. A person on the left sends an email; an AI agent below them
 *     sends a request. Their two lines meet at a single port on the inbox, because the post's
 *     first claim is that there is one inbox for both.
 *  2. Whatever arrives becomes a typed item with a state. Each thing that comes through the door
 *     lands as a row — Message, Booking, Quote, Order, the four types the post names, each in its
 *     own colour — and every row carries its own state from the moment it lands. The booking
 *     confirms itself (a rule, the bike shop's small booking with a free slot); the quote waits
 *     for a person — "Needs you", the homepage hero's word for it — with the amber dot that
 *     breathes, as the plumber's rule sends it. This is the point of the post, so it owns the
 *     longest beat: 6 s of a 13.9 s loop.
 *  3. The owner answers the rest, or their own AI does. "You" answers the message; "Your AI"
 *     confirms the order. Each reply travels in through the right-hand port and the row's state
 *     turns over. The quote is left waiting on purpose: at a glance you can see what still needs
 *     an answer, which is the sentence the post spends on states.
 *  4. The code is open. The footer of the inbox draws itself: a code mark, "Open source" and "Run
 *     it yourself", and the card's edge takes the palm tint. The post says the business can run it
 *     itself today and that hosting for others is not offered yet, so hosting is not drawn.
 *
 * Nothing here claims a shipped feature the post does not: no status chip, no count, no logo, no
 * interface chrome. The words are the post's own — the four types, a handful of states, "Open
 * source", "Run it yourself" — and it is an explanation, drawn.
 *
 * Built the way CoverReceipts.astro and HeroFlow.astro are built. The scene ships as inline SVG,
 * so the box is never empty and a throw leaves a finished picture; that markup is the composed
 * frame at t = 11.7 s and the live clock starts there, so there is no mount flicker, and a visitor
 * gets the whole picture for 1.5 s before the loop clears it. Reduced motion is the same machine
 * stepped to that frame, carrying all four beats at once. It stops when nobody is looking, and
 * has no max-width of its own — aspect-ratio holds the box open.
 *
 * Two day inks are a step darker than the tokens: the Message and Order labels use #0b6a75 and
 * #9c4409 in light, as HeroFlow does, because the token values miss 4.5:1 on the glass at this
 * size. Night uses the host's own ink tokens.
 */
---

<figure
  class="sdo"
  role="img"
  aria-label="A person's email and an AI agent's request arrive through the same door of one inbox. Each becomes a typed item — a message, a booking, a quote, an order — with its own state: the booking confirms itself, the quote waits for a person, and the owner or the owner's own AI answers the rest. The inbox's code is open source, so a business can run it itself."
>
